<div class="tab {{tabs.battlefields.cssClass}}" data-group="primary" data-tab="battlefields">

{{! Its own tab rather than a panel inside Combat. A Battlefield is a thing the whole
    table is standing in rather than a number on one character, and the Combat tab is
    already a column of this character's own tracking values.

    Every rule here is about this character alone. What the rest of the map is like is
    the table's - nothing in this system has Squares to ask - so what is on this tab is
    set by the player, or by whatever else the table decides. }}
  <section class="panel">
    <h3 class="panel-title">
      Light Level
      <span class="panel-hint">Set by you, or by an effect</span>
    </h3>

    <div class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat" data-tooltip="What the light where you are standing is doing to you. Too bright and too dark cost the same: the table is symmetrical about Normal.">
        <em>Level</em>
        <select name="system.battlefield.lightLevel" data-dtype="Number">
          {{#each lightLevels as |level|}}
          <option value="{{level.value}}" {{#if level.chosen}}selected{{/if}}>{{level.label}}</option>
          {{/each}}
        </select>
      </div>
    </div>

    {{#if lightLevel.effect}}
    {{! What the chosen Level is doing, in the rulebook's own words. Drawn only when it is
        doing something: Normal says "no effect", and a row repeating that is a row to
        read past. }}
    <p class="tab-placeholder">{{lightLevel.effect}}</p>
    {{/if}}
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <h3 class="panel-title">What is the table's</h3>

    {{! Said here rather than left out. Both of these are about Squares, and this system
        measures no distances and draws no areas - so they are settled at the table and
        the answer is typed in above. }}
    <p class="tab-placeholder">
      <strong>Scale.</strong> Each Square has its Light Level worked out on its own, and
      an ARC may cast the whole Battlefield in darkness. Nothing here tracks Squares, so
      what the light is where you are standing is yours to set.
    </p>
    <p class="tab-placeholder">
      <strong>Light Sources.</strong> A Light Source raises or sets the Light Level of an
      area and presents an Area of Effect. Areas are the table's here as well: apply one
      by changing the Level above.
    </p>
  </section>
</div>
